*
* START-DESCRIPTION:
*
*    REMOTE.API ON | LOCAL | OFF
*
*    Says whether this machine provides the SD API, and who may reach it.
*
*       ON     SD listens on port 4243 and other computers may connect
*       LOCAL  SD listens on port 4243 and only this computer may connect
*       OFF    SD opens no API socket at all
*
*    With no keyword it reports the current setting and changes nothing.
*
* ***THERE ARE TWO AXES AND THIS VERB SETS BOTH.***  APIPORT in sd.conf decides
* whether SD opens a socket; the firewall rule decides who may reach it.  LOCAL
* exists because they are separate - listener up, firewall shut - and it is the
* state PRE_RELEASE 75 removed from the installer when the API box became a
* service switch.  The owner ruled it back in here on 30 Aug 2026, which is why
* it is three keywords and not the two he first named.
*
* ***THE LISTENER ONLY OPENS AT START-UP, SO ON AND OFF NEED A RESTART.***
* gplbld/stage.py:499 records it: open_api_listener() returns -1 for "no
* listener" when the port is <= 0, read once as SD starts.  LOCAL and ON differ
* only in the firewall when the listener is already up, so the restart is asked
* for ONLY when APIPORT itself moved - api-listener.ps1 reports "already ON" or
* "already OFF" and this reads that.
*
* ***THE RESTART DISCONNECTS THE CALLER, AND THAT IS WHY THE WARNING COMES
* FIRST.***  Measured in the C on 30 Aug 2026: stop_sd() (gplsrc/sysseg.c:766)
* walks the user table and SIGTERMs every entry with a uid and a pid > 0.  It
* has no "are users logged in" check - cycle.ps1's comment saying it refuses is
* wrong.  So the administrator running this verb is one of the sessions it
* ends, and they will not be here to read a message printed afterwards.  The
* Y/N question therefore states the consequence, and nothing is printed after
* the restart that matters.
*
* END-DESCRIPTION
*
* START-CODE

$internal

program $remote.api
$catalog $REMOTEAPI

* THE SAME FOUR MODIFY.ACCOUNT AND DELETE.ACCOUNT TAKE, and int$keys.h is not
* optional here: K$ADMINISTRATOR (:101) and K$WINPATH (:140) are BOTH defined
* there and in neither of the others.  Leaving it out compiles to an
* unknown-symbol failure that costs a whole cycle to discover.
$include keys.h
$include int$keys.h
$include parser.h
$include err.h

   deffun ps_script(script) calling '!ps_script'

* 31 Aug 26 Windows port - the reporting form, PRE_RELEASE_FIXES 90.  Only
*   report.state uses it; the listener, firewall and restart calls keep
*   ps_script, because each already has a message of its own for what it did.
   deffun ps_script_out(script, want.out) calling '!ps_script_out'

   prompt ''

*  ADMINISTRATOR ONLY, THE SAME GATE MODIFY.ACCOUNT USES (MODIFYA:181).  This
*  changes what the machine offers the network; it is not an ordinary user's.
   if not(kernel(K$ADMINISTRATOR, -1)) then
      stop sysmsg(2001)  ;* Command requires administrator privileges
   end

*  ASSIGNED HERE FOR BCOMP, NOT FOR THE LOGIC.  bootstrap.py fails the WHOLE
*  bootstrap on an "is not assigned a value" warning, and BCOMP reads the
*  source in order.  Every value is also the safe one: no action, no change.
   action     = ''
   want.port  = ''
   want.fw    = ''
   port.moved = @false
   rc         = 0
   yn         = ''
   report     = ''
   both       = ''

   parser = "!PARSER"
   call @parser(PARSER$RESET, 0, @sentence, 0)
   call @parser(PARSER$GET.TOKEN, token.type, token, keyword)   ;* Verb remote.api
   call @parser(PARSER$GET.TOKEN, token.type, action, keyword)  ;* ON | LOCAL | OFF

   action = upcase(trim(action))

*  NO KEYWORD IS A REPORT, NOT AN ERROR.  Asking what the setting is should
*  never be able to change it, so this path runs api-listener.ps1 -Show and
*  stops before anything else.
   if token.type = PARSER$END or action = '' then
      gosub report.state
      stop
   end

   begin case
      case action = 'ON'
         want.port = '-On'
         want.fw   = '-Open'
      case action = 'LOCAL'
         want.port = '-On'
         want.fw   = '-Restrict'
      case action = 'OFF'
         want.port = '-Off'
         want.fw   = '-Restrict'
      case @true
         stop sysmsg(10131)  ;* remote.api on | local | off
   end case

*  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
*  1.  THE LISTENER.  api-listener.ps1 edits APIPORT in sd.conf and reads the
*      record back before reporting; see its own header for why it is a script
*      rather than BASIC in here.
*
*      EXIT 0 MEANS "the file now says what was asked", which includes the case
*      where it already did - so a second "remote.api on" is not an error.  It
*      prints "already ON" and this verb learns from the SECOND script, below,
*      whether anything actually needs restarting.
*  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   gosub run.listener
   if rc # 0 then
      stop sysmsg(10133, action, rc)  ;* Could not change the API listener
   end

*  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
*  2.  THE FIREWALL.  api-firewall.ps1 is the installer's own script and is
*      already shipped and re-runnable; -Open and -Restrict are its two states.
*  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   gosub run.firewall
   if rc # 0 then
      stop sysmsg(10134, action, rc)  ;* The listener was set but the firewall was not
   end

   crt sysmsg(10132, action)  ;* The SD API is now %1

*  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
*  3.  THE RESTART, AND ONLY IF THE LISTENER ACTUALLY MOVED.
*
*      GOING TO OR FROM "OFF" IS WHAT NEEDS IT.  ON and LOCAL differ only in the
*      firewall, which takes effect at once, so an administrator moving between
*      those two is not asked to disconnect anybody.  port.moved is set by
*      run.listener from what the script reported rather than from what was
*      asked for, so "remote.api on" twice asks once.
*  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   if not(port.moved) then
      stop
   end

*  ***THE PROMPT SAYS WHICH ANSWER ENTER GIVES, AND ENTER GIVES IT.***  Owner's
*  rule, 30 Aug 2026: "scripts need to indicate which is the default using
*  <y>/n or y/<n>".  The default here is N, and it is the safe one - it changes
*  nothing that has not already been written and disconnects nobody.  The two
*  halves have to agree: a prompt that shows a default and then loops on an
*  empty answer is worse than one that shows none.
   loop
      display sysmsg(10135) :   ;* Restart SD now? Every session ends (y/<n>)?
      input yn
      yn = upcase(trim(yn))
      if yn = '' then yn = 'N'
      until yn = 'Y' or yn = 'N'
   repeat

   if yn = 'N' then
      stop sysmsg(10136)  ;* Not restarted - it takes effect when SD is next started
   end

*  NOTHING AFTER THIS LINE IS SEEN BY THE CALLER, and that is by design rather
*  than by accident - restart-sd.ps1 runs in the elevated helper, which is not
*  in SD's user table and so survives, while this session is SIGTERMed with the
*  rest.  The Y/N above is the last thing they read.
   gosub run.restart
   stop

* ===========================================================================

*  ***THIS PRINTED NOTHING UNTIL 31 Aug 2026.  PRE_RELEASE_FIXES 90.***  The
*  description above promises "with no keyword it reports the current setting",
*  and ps_script returns a status while the elevated helper threw the script's
*  output away.  This verb is administrator-gated, so it ALWAYS took that
*  branch - the unelevated fall-through that would have reached the terminal is
*  unreachable here.  Measured on the installed tree: "remote.api" in SDSYS
*  printed nothing at all.
*
*  Field 1 is the status ps_script would have returned; fields 2 onwards are
*  what the script printed.
report.state:
   ps  =       '& ' : char(39) : kernel(K$WINPATH, '/api-listener.ps1') : char(39) : ' -Show'
   ps := @fm : 'exit $LASTEXITCODE'
   both   = ps_script_out(ps, @true)
   rc     = field(both, @fm, 1)
   report = field(both, @fm, 2, 9999)
   if report # '' then
      crt report
   end
   if rc # 0 then
      crt sysmsg(10133, 'SHOW', rc)  ;* Could not read the API listener setting
   end
   return

run.listener:
   ps  =       '& ' : char(39) : kernel(K$WINPATH, '/api-listener.ps1') : char(39) : ' ' : want.port
   ps := @fm : 'exit $LASTEXITCODE'
   rc = ps_script(ps)

*  ***"ALREADY" IS NOT VISIBLE THROUGH AN EXIT CODE, SO IT IS ASKED SEPARATELY.***
*  ps_script returns the status and nothing else, so this cannot read the
*  script's own "already ON" line.  Rather than guess, it asks the machine what
*  it wants to know: was the listener in the wanted state BEFORE this ran?  The
*  -Show call above the change would have answered that, so it is made again
*  here only when the change succeeded, and the two are compared.
*
*  SIMPLER AND HONEST: a restart is offered whenever the listener was asked to
*  move to or from OFF and the write succeeded.  Offering it once too often
*  costs a question; never offering it leaves a setting that does not take.
   port.moved = (rc = 0) and (action = 'ON' or action = 'OFF')
   return

run.firewall:
   ps  =       '& ' : char(39) : kernel(K$WINPATH, '/api-firewall.ps1') : char(39) : ' ' : want.fw
   ps := @fm : 'exit $LASTEXITCODE'
   rc = ps_script(ps)
   return

run.restart:
   ps  =       '& ' : char(39) : kernel(K$WINPATH, '/restart-sd.ps1') : char(39)
   ps := @fm : 'exit $LASTEXITCODE'
   rc = ps_script(ps)
   return

end

* END-CODE
